  "updated": "2026-10-10T18:39:27.018905+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool start Saturday followed by a nice day with a \nmix of clouds and sun. \n\nOff too a cool start, especially where winds have become calm \nallowing for better radiational cooling; many locations have dropped \ninto the low 40s while the warmer urban centers remain in the mid to \nupper 50s. It will be a gradual warm-up with sunrise as high \npressure eventually becomes centered overhead providing a pleasant \nFall day. Mostly sunny skies will be filtered by high cirrus \nstreaming north ahead of an approaching weather system. Expect highs \nto reach into the mid 60s. Trended lows toward MOS guidance a bit \nSaturday night as winds will be calm, though high clouds will limit \nradiational cooling if they're widespread enough. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain arrives Sunday, likely exiting by Monday \nmorning but there remains uncertainty on how quickly rain clears.  \n\nThe main focus for the holiday weekend weather-wise is generally the \n24 hour period from 12pm Sunday to 12pm Monday. This, as a surge of \nmoisture makes its way north, the remnants of Hurricane Isaias. \nAfter lifting north up the MS Valley it will be pulled east by a \nnorthern stream trough and move over Southern New England, \nstrengthening as it exits the Mid-Atlantic coast and passes near the \n40/70 benchmark. This plume of anomalous moisture (PWATs approaching \n2 inches are 2-3 SD above normal) will interact with good dynamics \nto squeeze out a good 0.5 (north) to 1 (south) inches of rain in \nthis time period. Some locations could see closer to 1.5\" mainly \nalong the south coast where the core of a 40-50kt LLJ will move \noverhead. Could also see a maxima on the slopes of the \nBerkshires/Worcester Hills where orographics could help depending on \nthe exact track of the low. Ensemble guidance continues to suggest \nlow probabilities of 1+\" rainfall totals outside of the south coast \nwhere probabilities are in the 40-50% range. The short duration and \nmanageable totals do not suggest that we'd be concerned with flood \nthreat. The greatest uncertainty continue to be exactly when Sunday \nafternoon the rain arrives and how quickly it exits Monday. Of late, \nodds are increasing slightly that showers could linger Monday \nmorning, drying by the afternoon. We'll know more as we get into the \nhi-resolution guidance over the next 24 hours. Fortunately, the bulk \nof the aforementioned LLJ should be kept aloft by inverted \natmospheric profiles so we are not expecting damaging winds with \nthis system at this time. Small Craft Advisories will likely be \nneeded on the waters for gusts 25-30 kts. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Quick round of rain around Tuesday/Tuesday night, \nthen dry weather returns Wed-Thu  \n\nIt's but a brief respite from the rain on Monday as the mid level \nridging following Sunday night's system will quickly be ushered out \nas another shortwave swings through on Tuesday with PWATs quickly \nback up to 1.75\". As the surface low passes near or just to our \nnorth generating a 35-45 kt 850mb LLJ resulting in breezy and wet \nweather around Tuesday or Tuesday night. High pressure returns \nWednesday into Thursday ushering in a quiet and seasonable stretch.",
